WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.851 --> 00:00:03.289
A KPI is a number someone acts on.

2
00:00:04.021 --> 00:00:05.821
Otherwise it is a report figure.

3
00:00:06.851 --> 00:00:11.797
A team steers on three to seven numbers; a wall of forty gets read by nobody.

4
00:00:12.811 --> 00:00:15.737
A lagging indicator measures the result afterwards,

5
00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:18.175
like last month's on-time delivery.

6
00:00:19.169 --> 00:00:24.765
A leading indicator moves first and shows what to do, like materials ready in time.

7
00:00:25.771 --> 00:00:26.874
Lagging: did you win?

8
00:00:27.588 --> 00:00:28.645
Leading: will you win?

9
00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.636
Kaplan and Norton's balanced scorecard: financial, customer, internal process,

10
00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.146
and learning and growth.

11
00:00:36.872 --> 00:00:39.449
Financial alone doesn't say where you are heading.

12
00:00:40.803 --> 00:00:44.553
A parts manufacturer gets complaints about late deliveries.

13
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.124
On the board: on-time delivery.

14
00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:50.438
Step one: write the definition down.

15
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:55.938
Against the confirmed date, per order line, zero days late; one owner.

16
00:00:56.962 --> 00:01:00.167
Also a fixed source, frequency and direction.

17
00:01:00.909 --> 00:01:05.112
Many arguments: two people measuring different things under one name.

18
00:01:06.074 --> 00:01:11.125
Step two: leading numbers for every week, like schedule adherence at the bottleneck.

19
00:01:12.174 --> 00:01:15.854
Step three: one number alone gets pushed at the cost of others,

20
00:01:16.005 --> 00:01:19.256
so paired indicators: first-pass yield alongside.

21
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.259
Dashboards grow every year, and leading numbers are hard to find.

22
00:01:25.301 --> 00:01:29.480
Ask of every number: when this went red, what did someone do differently?

23
00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:36.399
A KPI tree breaks a goal into the numbers that cause it, topped by, say, lead time.

24
00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:39.536
Each level asks: what drives this?

25
00:01:40.271 --> 00:01:43.418
That gives the drivers, down to what a team controls.

26
00:01:44.421 --> 00:01:46.383
A math tree computes exactly:

27
00:01:46.511 --> 00:01:51.294
good output is hours times running share times parts per hour times good share.

28
00:01:52.315 --> 00:01:55.844
A logic tree names the main drivers, no formula.

29
00:01:56.553 --> 00:01:59.978
Use math where you can: it shows each branch's worth.

30
00:02:00.986 --> 00:02:05.363
Each branch an owner: planning, maintenance, engineering, quality.

31
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.920
The last three are the OEE factors.

32
00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.900
Check with data: a branch that hardly varies won't explain the gap.

33
00:02:14.598 --> 00:02:16.583
Stop where a team can act weekly.

34
00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:22.433
A grid operator: request to connection takes 26 weeks on average,

35
00:02:22.618 --> 00:02:24.081
five steps in a row.

36
00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:27.690
Lead time is their sum: a math tree.

37
00:02:28.369 --> 00:02:32.212
Big branches: waiting for a crew, and design and permits.

38
00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:36.320
Too few crews, or jobs bounced back, not ready?

39
00:02:37.015 --> 00:02:40.916
Each step an owner and a weekly number, like the readiness check.

40
00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:43.459
Cascading is not copying.

41
00:02:44.195 --> 00:02:46.830
Lead time down twenty percent: a slogan.

42
00:02:47.553 --> 00:02:52.082
Jobs on the planned day, 70 to 85 percent: a team number.

43
00:02:53.087 --> 00:02:58.544
Small gains multiply: four branches ten percent better, the top about 46 percent.

44
00:02:59.284 --> 00:03:04.230
What goes wrong: drawn once, overlapping branches, drivers nobody owns.

45
00:03:05.533 --> 00:03:10.409
Goal-setting theory, from Locke and Latham and some 400 studies: specific,

46
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.371
hard goals beat do your best.

47
00:03:13.391 --> 00:03:16.352
Given goal acceptance, skills and feedback.

48
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.447
Otherwise: a wish.

49
00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:20.032
Too hard?

50
00:03:20.847 --> 00:03:23.749
Then people give up, or overstate their results,

51
00:03:23.923 --> 00:03:26.036
especially when they just fell short.

52
00:03:27.092 --> 00:03:28.392
Start from the baseline,

53
00:03:28.543 --> 00:03:33.338
not last year plus ten percent: a target close to your best weeks is believable.

54
00:03:34.071 --> 00:03:35.406
Beyond anything seen?

55
00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:37.207
Say what changes.

56
00:03:38.597 --> 00:03:41.395
Use a target range, because results vary.

57
00:03:42.103 --> 00:03:45.133
And apart from it a trigger: where you look for a cause.

58
00:03:46.137 --> 00:03:51.049
A machining cell: eight weeks of scrap, about 4.1 percent on average.

59
00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:53.585
Management wants 1 percent.

60
00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:56.326
That's below any week ever.

61
00:03:57.063 --> 00:04:02.764
Proposal: 3.0 to 4.0 percent, aiming for 3.5; above 5, look for the cause.

62
00:04:03.756 --> 00:04:05.208
Keep the ambition visible:

63
00:04:05.312 --> 00:04:09.735
root-cause work on the biggest defect could make 1 percent realistic later.

64
00:04:10.765 --> 00:04:14.144
Involve the team; people defend targets they helped set.

65
00:04:14.895 --> 00:04:18.146
Ask: what must change to make our best week a normal week?

66
00:04:19.429 --> 00:04:23.481
Goodhart's law, in Strathern's words: when a measure becomes a target,

67
00:04:23.644 --> 00:04:25.374
it ceases to be a good measure.

68
00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:30.020
Pressure on one number, and people find the cheapest way.

69
00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:33.578
Gaming: moving the number, not what it should show.

70
00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:38.456
Shifting work just outside the period, redefining what counts,

71
00:04:38.561 --> 00:04:41.568
fudging, or cherry-picking: the easy jobs first.

72
00:04:42.622 --> 00:04:45.339
A perverse incentive encourages the opposite.

73
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:51.132
Steve Kerr, 1975: rewarding A while hoping for B.

74
00:04:52.099 --> 00:04:55.001
Wells Fargo: under aggressive sales targets,

75
00:04:55.176 --> 00:04:58.380
staff opened millions of accounts nobody asked for.

76
00:04:59.127 --> 00:05:02.111
Nobody designed fraud; the pressure made it easy.

77
00:05:03.406 --> 00:05:06.623
Spot it: the number improves, nothing else does.

78
00:05:07.322 --> 00:05:10.933
Jumps right before the deadline, results just above the threshold.

79
00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:16.824
A grid operator ties a team bonus to outages restored within 4 hours:

80
00:05:16.929 --> 00:05:19.401
from 88 to 97 percent.

81
00:05:20.424 --> 00:05:25.173
But outages returning within 30 days go from 6 to 14 per hundred.

82
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.967
Are temporary fixes stopping the clock?

83
00:05:29.046 --> 00:05:33.145
Check, don't accuse: follow-up work orders, and ask the crews.

84
00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:38.171
Then the system: pair with repeat outages, bonus off that one number.

85
00:05:39.173 --> 00:05:42.632
Gaming mostly comes from a bad system, not bad people.

86
00:05:43.353 --> 00:05:46.012
Ask: how would you hit this without improving anything?
